1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,271
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,271 --> 00:00:08,259
Time to grade this morning's call — the part most shows skip.

3
00:00:08,259 --> 00:00:15,210
Bristol-Myers Squibb, ticker BMY never reached the trigger, so no position was taken.

4
00:00:15,210 --> 00:00:18,808
The entry did its quiet job: it kept us out.

5
00:00:18,808 --> 00:00:23,744
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

6
00:00:23,744 --> 00:00:29,153
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

7
00:00:29,118 --> 00:00:33,675
On the day, the book is up 156 dollars.

8
00:00:33,675 --> 00:00:37,999
Astronics (ATRO) leads, +2.1 percent.

9
00:00:37,999 --> 00:00:44,542
State Street SPDR S&P MIDCAP 400 ETF (MDY) lags at -0.6.

10
00:00:44,542 --> 00:00:50,852
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

11
00:00:50,852 --> 00:00:53,479
Today's lesson is noise versus signal.

12
00:00:53,479 --> 00:00:57,143
Daily moves are weather; the weekly trend is climate.

13
00:00:57,143 --> 00:01:00,185
The system never exits on a scary afternoon.

14
00:01:00,185 --> 00:01:05,716
It acts only on weekly closes, because that is where the real information lives.

15
00:01:05,666 --> 00:01:13,833
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

16
00:01:13,833 --> 00:01:21,566
12 positions, led by MetLife, ticker MET, Target, ticker TGT, BOK Financial, ticker BOKF.

17
00:01:21,566 --> 00:01:33,382
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

18
00:01:33,344 --> 00:01:38,143
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

19
00:01:38,143 --> 00:01:40,543
Every morning OROS proposes.

20
00:01:40,543 --> 00:01:51,083
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

21
00:01:51,083 --> 00:01:55,025
Inside the book today: 7 positions up, 5 down.

22
00:01:55,025 --> 00:01:57,425
Best, ATRO at +2.06 percent.

23
00:01:57,425 --> 00:01:59,053
Worst, DE at -0.87.

24
00:01:59,053 --> 00:02:04,024
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

25
00:02:04,024 --> 00:02:11,137
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -8.41% per trade over 174 graded positions.

26
00:02:11,137 --> 00:02:18,164
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 25% position size.

27
00:02:18,164 --> 00:02:23,306
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

28
00:02:23,306 --> 00:02:35,646
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 226 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 25% until the record earns it back.

29
00:02:35,620 --> 00:02:37,854
Over to the sports desk.

30
00:02:37,854 --> 00:02:47,625
Day 18 of 18 in the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

31
00:02:47,625 --> 00:02:53,861
In first place, the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, at 104,324.

32
00:02:53,861 --> 00:02:59,352
Then the index fund, in gray, 1.9 thousand back at 102,464.

33
00:02:59,352 --> 00:03:06,611
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 4.1 thousand back at 100,181.

34
00:03:06,611 --> 00:03:08,193
That's the final.

35
00:03:08,193 --> 00:03:11,264
The full season review is coming.

36
00:03:11,264 --> 00:03:16,103
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

37
00:03:16,103 --> 00:03:17,965
Nothing is restated.

38
00:03:17,965 --> 00:03:26,872
On screen, three equity lines grown from the same starting dollar: the frozen first book, the live book, and the index fund.

39
00:03:26,834 --> 00:03:34,169
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

40
00:03:34,169 --> 00:03:36,411
Nothing here is financial advice.

41
00:03:36,411 --> 00:03:39,399
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
